body:
The `list[Unknown]` revealed there is actually a limitation of multi-inference diagnostics (see https://github.com/astral-sh/ty/issues/4871), we infer the correct type for the list internally.

The idea of this PR is if we infer a union for a given type variable during fixpoint iteration, a single member of that union should be able to partially specialize another generic call argument so long as the call is compatible with the full outer union. A consequence of this is that, as in your example, a union member like `None` may provide no useful type context to a nested call, and yet contribute a successful narrowing attempt, as the fact that the result was already inferred in a previous round gives us a basis to validate it despite coming from what we would otherwise consider an unsuccessful narrowing attempt.

It is actually possible that such an inference attempt, effectively without type context, does form a subset of the full union, and thus allow us to widen the nested call to a type compatible with its sibling arguments, despite its return type being disjoint with the specific member being narrowed to. For example, this snippet errors on main but passes with this PR.

```py
def index_of[T](items: list[T], item: T) -> int:
    return items.index(item)

def pack[T](xs: list[T]) -> tuple[list[T]]:
    return (xs,)

def _(rows: list[tuple[list[int] | list[str]] | None]):
    index_of(rows, pack([1]))
```

Note that this is whole process is somewhat hand-wavy (and will hopefully go away in the future), and the reason some of these examples work seems somewhat accidental, but for now it does seem to provide meaningfully better results.

(I'll try to improve the documentation and add the snippet above as a regression test.)

