body:
The new write contract recognizes `classmethod(...)` wrappers, but `target.is_method` excludes them, so equivalent definitions end up with different override checks:

```py
from typing import Callable

def impl(cls: type) -> object:
    return object()

class Base:
    f = classmethod(impl)

class Child(Base):
    f: Callable[[], str]
```

This now gets `invalid-mutable-override` on `Child.f`. The same subclass annotation passes if `Base.f` is written with `@classmethod`, and both forms pass on main. I think we should consistently apply the same write exemption to both forms?

