body:
This fast path rejects a valid call when we bind a classmethod’s underlying function to a class. The parent accepts this example, and it returns `42` on CPython 3.13.

```python
from typing import Protocol

class P[T](Protocol):
    @classmethod
    def f(cls) -> int:
        return 42

class C(P[int]):
    pass

assert P.f.__func__.__get__(C)() == 42
```

`P.f.__func__` binds as an ordinary function, but its first parameter still has the implicit annotation `type[Self]`. Here `typing_self_type` gives us the class object `C`. Substituting that for `Self` makes ty expect `<class '_ProtocolMeta'>` at the call.

Might be a good idea to include "Meta" in these assertions to ensure they are matching the invalid-override we expect them to match (not e.g. due to an `id` field mismatch or something.)

FWIW every type checker errors on this non-Django equivalent, so I don't think our basic rule here is overly pedantic:

```py
class AbstractHasTitle:
    class Meta:
        abstract = True

class AbstractHasId:
    class Meta:
        abstract = True

# error: [invalid-attribute-override] 
class Book1(AbstractHasTitle, AbstractHasId):
    pass

# error: [invalid-attribute-override]
class Book2(AbstractHasId, AbstractHasTitle):
    pass
```

Pyrefly and zuban don't error on the Django version, I presume due to some special-casing of `Meta` on Django models; I guess we could do this too.

We might need some special handling for this case:

```python
from django.db import models

class Parent(models.Model):
    class Meta:
        abstract = True

class Child(Parent):
    class Meta(Parent.Meta):
        ordering = ["id"]
```

Django clears `Parent.Meta.abstract` when it creates `Parent`, so `Child` is not considered abstract and gets an integer primary key. 

[Django's example of Meta inheritance](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/models/#meta-inheritance)

